## Muda (waste): remove it when you find it
Muda is: duplicated logic, a fact that has more than one home, dead code, dead fields, an
abstraction that you add before it is necessary, drift, and history that git can show. The
**`engineering-principles`** skill (`waste-identification` and `ssot-enforcement`) contains the
types of muda and the audit method. Refer to that skill. Do not write its content again here.
Use these rules:
- **Cite-or-silence:** When you identify muda, give the `file:line` where it occurs. Do not make
  a "consolidation" only to look useful. **Do not add unnecessary process.** Process that you add
  before it is necessary is also muda.
- **Poka-yoke:** Delete the copy of a fact. Get the value from its home. Do not make a duplicate.
  The `engineering-principles` skill, section Process-Level Poka-yoke, gives the rules.
- **Forcing functions:** `adr-lint` checks the decision log. The muda-review CI is advisory. It
  adds one comment with its findings. It does not stop the merge. Use `/retrospect` at the end of
  each session. You can also use it at other times (ADR 0081). A result with no findings is
  valid. Do not write a line that only says that all is satisfactory.
- **Two-why (ADR 0081):** Before you repair a failure of a gate, CI, or verifier, answer two
  questions. Is this failure one instance, or is it a class of failures? Which rung of the ladder
  must find this type of failure before this point? The answer "my error" does not complete the
  chain.
